_CF_WARNING = """Installs and manages a <b>Cloudflare Tunnel (cloudflared)</b> as a persistent,
  <b>outbound-only</b> connector, so a local service (the Dashboard 8989, sysmon 9999,
  instmon 8990, or this 44helper) can be reached over the internet with <b>no inbound
  port-forward and no public IP</b>. Choose a method: <b>token</b> (default) installs a
  persistent, dashboard-managed cloudflared.service from your Zero Trust tunnel token; <b>quick</b>
  runs a throwaway TryCloudflare test tunnel (a random *.trycloudflare.com, foreground, dies on
  Ctrl-C). Ingress hostnames and Access policies live in the Cloudflare
  <a href="https://one.dash.cloudflare.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zero Trust dashboard</a>,
  not in a local file.
  <br><br><b>Security &mdash; it BYPASSES the firewall.</b> A Cloudflare Tunnel is the opposite of
  what the Firewall/Ports tabs reason about: nothing opens inbound, so firewalld never sees the
  exposure and this tool <b>cannot flag it on the Ports tab</b>. Publishing a service that has no
  auth of its own (the dashboard on 8989 has none; instmon's auth is opt-in) over a public
  Cloudflare hostname makes it world-reachable. <b>Only tunnel a service that has its own auth,
  and/or put a Cloudflare Access policy in front of the hostname</b> (same dashboard) before
  exposing anything. For the token method the ingress mapping lives server-side in Cloudflare, so
  this tool can never enumerate what a running connector exposes &mdash; it can only say "connector
  active &rarr; something may be public, check the dashboard."
  <br><br><b>WireGuard:</b> works in <b>any</b> combination &mdash; recommended with <b>no WireGuard
  or a split tunnel</b> (cloudflared's edge egress takes the physical default route, clean). Under a
  <b>full 44Net tunnel</b> (AllowedIPs=0.0.0.0/0) it still works, but the edge connection (7844/udp)
  rides wg0 through the 44Net gateway, which must NAT outbound and pass 7844, and it drops if the
  tunnel drops &mdash; discouraged. The pre-flight step classifies wg0 and warns; no combination is
  blocked.
  <br><br>Docs:
  <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/networks/connectors/cloudflare-tunnel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> &middot;
  <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/policies/access/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Access policies</a> &middot;
  <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/connections/connect-networks/do-more-with-tunnels/trycloudflare/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TryCloudflare (quick)</a>.
  Uninstall runs the non-optional steps automatically after a double-confirm; the optional
  credential-removal step is left for you to run by hand. BENCH-ONLY: the apt repo/key, the
  full-tunnel egress path, and the quick tunnel actually printing a reachable URL are not
  exercisable from the build sandbox &mdash; verify on real hardware. Check firewall posture on the
  Ports tab after install (noting the enumeration limitation above)."""

